b=Batch('TS',1)
q('M','Types','unknown and any','Which type requires narrowing before arbitrary property access?','unknown','unknown safely represents an unvalidated value. any disables ordinary checking for its uses and can spread unchecked operations through the code.','Look for a checked top type.','Which type forces proof before use?','ts:handbook/2/functions.html#unknown',wrong=['any','never','{}'])
q('B','Nullability','Optional string','Under strictNullChecks, why is this unsafe, and what expression returns zero when the name is absent?','name may be undefined. Use: return name?.length ?? 0;','The optional parameter includes undefined.~Direct length access has no guarantee of a string.~Optional access plus a nullish fallback handles absence while preserving a real length of zero.','The parameter is optional.','Narrow or guard before property access.','ts:handbook/2/everyday-types.html#optional-properties','function length(name?: string) {\n  return name.length;\n}')
q('C','Types','Structural compatibility','Why can a value with extra fields often be assigned to an interface that requires only id and name?','TypeScript generally checks structural compatibility. The value must provide the required members with compatible types. Fresh object literals receive additional excess-property checks in certain assignment contexts.','Types are not nominal class identities by default. Excess-property checks are a useful literal check, not a universal exact-object-type guarantee.','Compare shapes, not declarations.','Is this a fresh literal or an existing variable?','ts:handbook/type-compatibility.html')
q('M','Unions','Safe union operations','For value: string | number, which operation is valid without first narrowing?','value.toString()','Both members support toString. String-only and number-only methods need narrowing before they can be called safely.','Use the common member set.','Which method exists on both alternatives?','ts:handbook/2/everyday-types.html#working-with-union-types',wrong=['value.toUpperCase()','value.toFixed(2)','value.trim()'])
q('W','Narrowing','typeof guard','Write format(value: string | number): string, uppercasing strings and formatting numbers with two decimals.','function format(value: string | number): string {\n  if (typeof value === "string") {\n    return value.toUpperCase();\n  }\n  return value.toFixed(2);\n}\n','typeof narrows the first branch to string.~The remaining branch is number.~Each branch uses a valid method and returns a string; no assertion is needed.','Use a runtime type test.','What remains after the string branch returns?','ts:handbook/2/narrowing.html#typeof-type-guards','// Implement format(value: string | number): string.')
q('C','Types','never meaning','What does never represent, and how can it help with exhaustiveness?','never represents values that cannot occur. A fully handled union can narrow to never. Assigning an unhandled variant to never reveals a missing case at compile time.','never is not equivalent to unknown or void. It models impossibility, while void describes an ignored or absent meaningful return value.','Think of an empty set of values.','What type should remain after every case is handled?','ts:handbook/2/narrowing.html#the-never-type')
q('B','Assertions','Runtime validation','Why does this assertion not prove the API response has a name?','Type assertions are erased and perform no runtime validation. Validate the unknown response shape before treating it as User.','The network can return any JSON value.~as User only changes the compiler’s view.~A missing name can still fail at runtime; use real checks or a schema validator at the boundary.','Compilation does not inspect future JSON.','What JavaScript does the assertion emit?','ts:handbook/2/everyday-types.html#type-assertions','type User = { name: string };\nconst user = (await response.json()) as User;\nconsole.log(user.name.toUpperCase());')
q('M','Literals','const assertion','What does as const do to this object’s mode property at the type level?','It becomes readonly with the literal type "dark".','A const assertion prevents literal widening and marks object properties readonly in the inferred type. It does not call Object.freeze at runtime.','Separate static typing from runtime freezing.','Does the type stay a specific literal?','ts:handbook/release-notes/typescript-3-4.html#const-assertions','const settings = { mode: "dark" } as const;',wrong=['It becomes mutable string.','It becomes a runtime getter.','It becomes the never type.'])
q('C','Collections','ReadonlyArray contract','What does ReadonlyArray<T> prevent, and what does it not guarantee?','It prevents mutation through that typed array reference. It does not freeze the runtime array. Mutable aliases and mutable element objects can still change underlying data.','readonly is a compile-time access restriction. Deep immutability and ownership require additional design beyond a readonly array annotation.','Follow aliases to the same object.','Are the elements themselves immutable?','ts:handbook/2/objects.html#the-readonlyarray-type')
q('W','Functions','Optional parameter handling','Write greet(name?: string): string returning Hello, Guest only when name is undefined. Preserve an empty string.','function greet(name?: string): string {\n  return `Hello, ${name ?? "Guest"}`;\n}\n','The optional parameter includes undefined.~The nullish operator chooses Guest only for absence in this type domain.~An empty string is preserved, unlike with ||.','Absence and empty text differ.','Which fallback preserves falsy strings?','ts:handbook/2/functions.html#optional-parameters','// Implement greet(name?: string): string.')
q('U','Types','unknown boundary','Follow-up to TS-001: why prefer unknown over any for an unvalidated parsed payload?','It forces consumers to establish the shape before use. Narrowing documents that proof. any would allow unchecked operations to spread beyond the boundary.','unknown is not validation by itself. Runtime checks are still needed, but the type system requires them before unsafe property access.','Make uncertainty visible.','Which operations should require evidence?','ts:handbook/2/functions.html#unknown')
q('W','Generics','Preserve input type','Implement identity<T>(value: T): T without assertions or any.','function identity<T>(value: T): T {\n  return value;\n}\n// O(1) time and auxiliary space.','The type parameter captures the caller’s input type.~The return uses the same type parameter.~Returning unknown would lose the input/output relationship, while any would skip checking.','Relate input and output with one parameter.','No conversion is required.','ts:handbook/2/generics.html#hello-world-of-generics','// Implement identity<T>(value: T): T.')
q('W','Generics','Key constraints','Implement getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(object: T, key: K): T[K].','function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(object: T, key: K): T[K] {\n  return object[key];\n}\n','keyof T limits keys to the object’s known property keys.~K preserves the specific chosen key.~T[K] returns that property’s type instead of a broad union.','Constrain the key using the object type.','How do you index a type by a key?','ts:handbook/2/generics.html#using-type-parameters-in-generic-constraints','// Implement getProperty with a key tied to T.')
q('M','Generics','Constraint versus exact type','For T extends { length: number }, what can the generic function safely assume?','Every accepted T has a numeric length property.','A constraint guarantees required structure without discarding additional information from T. It does not restrict T to arrays or make every property available.','A constraint is a minimum shape.','Could a string also satisfy it?','ts:handbook/2/generics.html#generic-constraints',wrong=['T must be an array of numbers.','T has no properties other than length.','T can be accessed as any without checking.'])
q('B','Narrowing','Truthiness loses empty strings','The function must preserve empty strings. What narrowing condition should replace the truthiness check?','Use: if (value !== null) { return value; }','The union is string or null.~A truthiness check rejects both null and the empty string.~Explicit null comparison narrows to string while preserving valid empty input.','Falsy is broader than null.','Which one union member should be excluded?','ts:handbook/2/narrowing.html#truthiness-narrowing','function normalize(value: string | null) {\n  if (value) return value;\n  return "missing";\n}')
q('W','Unions','Discriminated result','Write readResult(result: Result): string for the shown union, returning data on success and error on failure.','type Result =\n  | { ok: true; data: string }\n  | { ok: false; error: string };\n\nfunction readResult(result: Result): string {\n  return result.ok ? result.data : result.error;\n}\n','The literal ok property distinguishes union members.~The true branch exposes data.~The false branch exposes error; optional fields on one broad interface would weaken that relationship.','Use the shared discriminant.','Which fields exist in each branch?','ts:handbook/2/narrowing.html#discriminated-unions','type Result =\n  | { ok: true; data: string }\n  | { ok: false; error: string };')
q('C','Narrowing','User-defined predicates','What responsibility does a function returning value is User take on?','It promises that a true result proves the User shape. TypeScript uses that promise to narrow callers. The function must perform sufficient runtime checks because an incorrect predicate can make later code unsafe.','The compiler does not derive every declared predicate’s truth from its implementation. A type predicate is a contract, not automatic schema validation.','A predicate is trusted evidence.','Does its implementation check every required field?','ts:handbook/2/narrowing.html#using-type-predicates')
q('W','Validation','Unknown object guard','Write isUser(value: unknown): value is User for User = { name: string }.','type User = { name: string };\n\nfunction isUser(value: unknown): value is User {\n  return typeof value === "object" && value !== null\n    && "name" in value && typeof value.name === "string";\n}\n','Exclude primitives and null.~Check that name exists before reading its type.~The string test establishes the required shape. This structural guard permits extra properties and inherited name properties by contract.','Narrow before accessing a field.','Remember typeof null is object.','ts:handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-9.html#unlisted-property-narrowing-with-the-in-operator','type User = { name: string };\n// Implement isUser(value: unknown): value is User.')
q('M','Utility types','Partial','What does Partial<User> change?','It makes each top-level User property optional.','Partial is a mapped utility over the top-level keys. It does not recursively make nested properties optional or validate patches at runtime.','Consider the depth of the transformation.','Does it recurse into object fields?','ts:handbook/utility-types.html#partialtype',wrong=['It makes every nested field optional recursively.','It removes every readonly modifier.','It returns a runtime proxy for User.'])
q('W','Utility types','Public projection type','Define PublicUser from User, retaining only id and name, using a utility type.','type User = { id: string; name: string; passwordHash: string };\ntype PublicUser = Pick<User, "id" | "name">;','Pick selects the named properties at the type level.~The resulting type describes only the public fields.~It does not remove passwordHash from an existing runtime object; actual serialization needs a projection.','Select a subset of keys.','Types do not strip runtime data.','ts:handbook/utility-types.html#picktype-keys','type User = { id: string; name: string; passwordHash: string };\n// Define PublicUser.')
q('C','satisfies','Validation without broad annotation','Why use satisfies for a configuration object instead of annotating the variable with a broad Record type?','It checks assignability to the required shape. It can preserve useful specific inferred property types. It does not cast an incompatible value into validity or perform runtime validation.','An annotation can widen the accessible type to the annotated contract. satisfies still supplies contextual typing, so it is not a promise that inference is completely unaffected.','Separate checking from replacing the variable’s type.','Which property-specific operations should remain available?','ts:handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-9.html#the-satisfies-operator')
q('B','Overloads','Implementation signature visibility','Why does combine accept one or three arguments but reject two, despite optional implementation parameters?','Only the overload signatures are public call signatures. Add an explicit two-argument overload if that call is part of the intended API.','Callers are checked against declared overloads.~The implementation signature exists to implement them and is not an additional callable overload.~Optional implementation parameters do not automatically expose every arity.','Separate public overloads from implementation.','Which signatures can callers see?','ts:handbook/2/functions.html#overload-signatures-and-the-implementation-signature','function combine(a: string): string;\nfunction combine(a: string, b: string, c: string): string;\nfunction combine(a: string, b?: string, c?: string) {\n  return a + (b ?? "") + (c ?? "");\n}\ncombine("a", "b");')
q('W','React typing','Native button props','Write a typed Button accepting normal button props plus a required label. Use ComponentPropsWithoutRef.','import type { ComponentPropsWithoutRef } from "react";\n\ntype ButtonProps = ComponentPropsWithoutRef<"button"> & {\n  label: string;\n};\n\nexport function Button({ label, ...props }: ButtonProps) {\n  return <button {...props}>{label}</button>;\n}\n','Derive the native element prop contract.~Remove label before spreading DOM props.~The wrapper keeps native event and disabled typing; this specific API intentionally does not expose a ref.','Reuse React’s element prop types.','Which custom prop must stay off the DOM node?','react:learn/typescript#typing-component-props','// Write Button in a .tsx file with React type definitions installed.')
q('W','React typing','Input change event','Write handleChange(event: ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>): void that logs the current input value.','import type { ChangeEvent } from "react";\n\nfunction handleChange(event: ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>): void {\n  console.log(event.currentTarget.value);\n}\n','The generic event type identifies the input element.~currentTarget is the element owning the handler.~Its value is a string; number inputs still need explicit conversion when numeric data is required.','Tie the event to the element type.','Which target is guaranteed to own this handler?','react:learn/typescript#typing-dom-events','// Implement handleChange for a React input onChange.')
q('B','React typing','Empty array state inference','Why can adding a User fail here, and what explicit Hook type fixes it?','An empty array can infer an unusably narrow element type. Use: const [users, setUsers] = useState<User[]>([]);','The initializer contains no User element for inference.~The explicit generic supplies the intended array element type.~Future updates are then checked against User rather than requiring unsafe assertions.','The initializer provides no element evidence.','Specify the intended state domain.','react:learn/typescript#typing-usestate','type User = { id: string };\nconst [users, setUsers] = useState([]);\nsetUsers([{ id: "a" }]);')
q('C','Configuration','strictNullChecks','What changes when strictNullChecks is enabled?','null and undefined are treated as distinct types. Potentially absent values must be handled before use where a definite value is required. This makes absence part of the checked contract.','The option does not add runtime checks. A non-null assertion can silence the compiler without making an absent value safe.','Absence becomes explicit in types.','Who performs the runtime guard?','ts:handbook/2/everyday-types.html#strictnullchecks')
q('M','Configuration','noUncheckedIndexedAccess','With noUncheckedIndexedAccess enabled, what is the type of values[index] for values: string[] and arbitrary numeric index?','string | undefined','An arbitrary index may be out of bounds. The option adds undefined to unchecked indexed access rather than assuming every numeric index exists.','Arrays can have missing indexes.','Is this index proven in range?','ts:handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-1.html#checked-indexed-accesses-nouncheckedindexedaccess',wrong=['Always string.','Always never.','Always null.'])
q('B','Modules','Type-only import','Under verbatimModuleSyntax, how should a type-only User import be written?','Use: import type { User } from "./types";','The declaration is used only in type positions.~import type explicitly marks it for erasure.~A normal import is preserved according to verbatimModuleSyntax rules and can require an actual runtime export.','Separate compile-time names from runtime values.','Which import form is erased?','ts:handbook/release-notes/typescript-5-0.html#verbatimmodulesyntax','import { User } from "./types";\nexport function label(user: User) {\n  return user.name;\n}')
q('C','Functions','void callback return','Why may a function returning a value be assigned to a callback type returning void?','A contextual void return means the caller ignores the returned value. It does not necessarily forbid the implementation from producing one. Callers typed with that contract cannot use the value as a meaningful result.','This rule supports callbacks such as array operations that discard results. It differs from explicitly declaring a function body with an incompatible annotated return.','Think about what the caller promises to use.','Is the return discarded?','ts:handbook/2/functions.html#return-type-void')
q('W','Unions','Exhaustive reducer','Implement next(state, action) for Action = { type: "inc" } | { type: "reset" }, with a never check for future unhandled variants.','type Action = { type: "inc" } | { type: "reset" };\n\nfunction next(state: number, action: Action): number {\n  switch (action.type) {\n    case "inc": return state + 1;\n    case "reset": return 0;\n    default: {\n      const exhaustive: never = action;\n      throw new Error(`Unexpected action: ${String(exhaustive)}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n','Each case handles one discriminant.~After all current variants, action narrows to never.~Adding a new variant makes the assignment fail until handled. External unvalidated actions still need runtime validation.','Use the impossible remainder.','What assignment fails when a new variant remains?','ts:handbook/2/narrowing.html#exhaustiveness-checking','type Action = { type: "inc" } | { type: "reset" };\n// Implement next(state, action): number.')
q('S','Utility types','Awaited results','A helper returns Promise<PromiseLike<User>> in its declared type. Which utility models the value obtained by awaiting it?','Use Awaited on the return type. It recursively unwraps promise-like values. Combine it with ReturnType when deriving the result from a function declaration.','A single indexed access into a Promise type does not model await semantics. Awaited also distributes appropriately across supported unions.','Model language await behavior.','Is one unwrap always enough?','ts:handbook/utility-types.html#awaitedtype')
q('M','Conditional types','Distributive conditional','For type Wrap<T> = T extends string ? T[] : never, what is Wrap<"a" | "b">?','"a"[] | "b"[]','A conditional with a naked type parameter distributes over union members. It evaluates each literal separately and unions the results. Tuple-wrapping the tested type can suppress distribution.','Evaluate the union one member at a time.','Is T naked on the left of extends?','ts:handbook/2/conditional-types.html#distributive-conditional-types','type Wrap<T> = T extends string ? T[] : never;\ntype Result = Wrap<"a" | "b">;',wrong=['("a" | "b")[] only.','never','string'])
q('W','Conditional types','infer array element','Define ElementOf<T> that extracts the element type of a readonly array and otherwise returns never.','type ElementOf<T> = T extends readonly (infer Item)[]\n  ? Item\n  : never;','The conditional tests for a readonly-array-compatible shape.~infer introduces the element type.~Nonarrays use never; readonly matching also accepts mutable arrays.','Match the container shape.','Where can infer capture the element?','ts:handbook/2/conditional-types.html#inferring-within-conditional-types','// Define ElementOf<T>.\n// ElementOf<readonly number[]> should be number.')
q('W','Mapped types','Remove optional modifiers','Define RequiredFields<T> using a mapped type that removes optional markers from all properties.','type RequiredFields<T> = {\n  [Key in keyof T]-?: T[Key];\n};','Iterate over keyof T.~The -? modifier removes optionality.~This is a type transformation, not runtime filling of missing properties. Existing explicit undefined unions follow the compiler’s optional-property rules.','Mapped modifiers can be removed.','Which modifier controls optionality?','ts:handbook/2/mapped-types.html#mapping-modifiers','// Define RequiredFields<T> without using the Required utility.')
q('C','Template literal types','Finite string contracts','How can template-literal types constrain event names such as user:created and user:deleted?','Combine literal unions inside a template-literal type. TypeScript expands the allowed combinations. Prefer generated declarations or runtime validation when the possible set becomes very large or comes from external input.','The type restricts compile-time assignments but does not validate arbitrary runtime strings. Large cross-products can also increase type-checking cost.','Build strings from finite pieces.','What combinations should be legal?','ts:handbook/2/template-literal-types.html')
